## Why This Is Asked

A concrete, beginner-friendly file upload hardening task that touches MIME handling and HTTP headers.

## Key Concepts

- MIME type detection by file signatures
- Content-Type vs Content-Disposition
- Whitelisting and secure defaults
- Server-side validation and test automation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js pseudo: detect mime by magic bytes
function detectMime(bytes) {
  if (bytesstartsWith([0xFF,0xD8])) return "image/jpeg";
  if (bytesstartsWith([0x89,0x50])) return "image/png";
  // ... simple mapping
  return "application/octet-stream";
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this with large files and streaming to avoid memory pressure?
- How would you extend to prevent mime-type sniffing by browsers?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-time trading platforms demand ultra-low latency combined with robust replay protection across distributed microservices. Token binding mechanisms reduce impersonation risks while maintaining performance requirements critical to trading operations.

## Key Concepts
- Token binding and mutual TLS (mTLS) for connection-specific authentication
- Short time-to-live (TTL) tokens with audience-scoping for service isolation
- WebSocket connection nonces and replay prevention mechanisms
- Global revocation propagation strategies
- Comprehensive observability and audit trail implementation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: validate token and bind to nonce
function authorize(token, nonce, ctx){
  if (!isValidToken(token)) return false;
  if (!bindsToTLS(token, ctx)) return false;
  const session = createSecureSession(token, nonce);
  return session.isValid() && !isReplayAttempt(nonce);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses end-to-end security hygiene for assets moving from client to cloud storage in a beginner-friendly way, focusing on practical steps rather than theory.

## Key Concepts
- Client and server validation, MIME vs magic bytes
- Malware scanning in upload pipelines
- Least-privilege IAM for storage buckets
- Short-lived signed URLs and expirations
- Logging redaction for PII and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// middleware to validate image uploads (Express + Multer-like placeholder)
const ALLOWED = ['image/jpeg','image/png','image/webp'];
function validateUpload(req, res, next) {
  const { 'content-type': ct } = req.headers;
  if (!ALLOWED.includes(ct)) return res.status(400).send('Unsupported type');
  const size = parseInt(req.headers['content-length'] || '0', 10);
  if (size > 5 * 1024 * 1024) return res.status(413).send('File too large');
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this pipeline end-to-end?
- What are common bypasses (e.g., content-type spoofing) and mitigations?
- How would you handle large files and retries in a serverless context?
